        <p>The Widow Rachel tilt.Antryn ocrupied a pretty pottage on the outskirts / the set I lettrett or village proper - a cottage belonging to the Mornington estate, All,t now owned by She young heir, Edgar. The family consisted of Itebelf and her daughter IWPI two coloured servants. the servanta were els the property of Mornington, hirt had been reoffered to rernain with the widow, three the death of her husband, withoun further pay for their services than their simple food anti care. (Jiving his horse to the boy Jake, who knew the animal well, the docror entered the dwelling with. out stopping to knock, meeting Mrs. St Aubyn in the hall, with a lighted candle in her hand. She was a woman ot live-and-forty ; with considerable nil erin the dark trove) tresses, and ,nany lines of GOV open her once beautiful face. 0, Doctor! lam glad you have corne, Clarice has been no anxious I I think she has lived only to see you. She has suffered more than can tell." Trovelyan esked no questions, but followed the mother directly to the bedside of her puttering daughter. '" ' " ' d "'-</p>
