        <p>--Eight menthe previous to the present time. Clarice had left her home ostensibly for the purpoee of visiting relations in New Jersey; but, mally, she had gone to Philadelphia for the ptiry.se of being ni.drie.l. A letter to her mother, two week. after led .IPparture, containe.f the following molten., "Monroe I love my husband very fleetly ; and he loves roe the sorer. I our sorry for Arthur, but he will not vete when he knows lam happv. I cannot now tell you my husband's nitre. I hove solvoinly sworn to him that I will not expocte it until ho shall give me wrinission to do so. An old, wealthy aunt, whose heir he Is to he, would disin- herit him If she knew it. 0, pray for me, Inaninlat soil brew he not a word to anybody of my marriage. NVait till I COlllO hoese, and my darling—my Hog I—with me." months had papPeil away after the writ itir of that letter, when the widowed mother re., .ved another. Iler daughter had henn bet - deserted. tipr liiisbonl hail fors:den her -had I..ft her, sick and penniless. lire. Mt. Auliyn hurried away to the tiiidant city, and found her child almost drool; but the irresence of a loving mother revive., her, and she was soon able to sit tip. The widow arran.yed with her servaid• at horne tr take rare of the house while she pent nearly three months with thin poor aufferer in Philadelphia, at the aspiration of which time phe brought her home to doe; for the doetors In the bbniker City hail assured her that the life of her child was but for a little longer time on this side the vale.</p>
        <p>And yet, in all these weary, suffering weeks, Clarinv hart not‘epoken the name of her betrayer. Every net of persuasion bad been bronght to beat upon her, but without avail. Had she been herself bad not her !spirit been broken and her mind weakened, she might have onnfewitul the troth; lout she was morbidly sensitive, and the necessity of maintaining her secret took the form of a noot. mania. title Mill Pile would tell ell at a proper time, but not now, O not now, please ;" Beyond the witiow's owo home, Herbert and Arthur Trevelyan nod Edger Mornington were the only persons known to have knowledge of Cleire's bitter experience. Others thought, as they hail been told. Ilint the dead girl hail contracted a melignant fever while visiting abroad ; mai they suspected no more. With reward to young Mornington, he hart been absent from home when Clarice went away, having left only two days previous to her departure, and having returned only twice during the first forlf months of her essence, and on those returns he had .pent only • day anti • night beneath Iris own roof, and then awn• again. Further. Edgar Morningten was seen in Philadelphia on three or four different osseasnms during these stone four month. One thing further and the thing which had fitted the doctor in his belief concerning Mornirignin's One day, while sitting liv Chance's bedside, bolding her wrist and counting the ;wises, she sank Into • tromtconscious state, and talked with herself.</p>
